# Copy to .env for `npm run db:test`.
#
# This file is only about the database that suite runs against. The apps have
# their own — see apps/intimacy/.env.example and apps/two-two-two/.env.example.
# Expo loads those itself, and nothing in this file reaches an app bundle.
#
# Variables already set in your shell win over this file, so a one-off
# `PGHOST=... npm run db:test` still overrides everything below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POINT THIS AT A LOCAL DATABASE ONLY.
#
# The suite runs `drop database if exists ... with (force)` and recreates it
# from supabase/migrations on every run. That is fine against a throwaway; it
# is not something to aim at a database anyone depends on. In particular, do
# not put your hosted Supabase connection string here — see the note at the
# bottom for how to check migrations against a real project instead.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret. If you ever find yourself pasting a
# service_role key or a hosted database password in here, stop: that is the
# wrong file and the wrong database.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Option A: the Supabase local stack (`supabase start`) -----------------
#
# Matches what the apps talk to in local development, and runs the same
# Postgres version as the hosted project. The credentials below are the fixed
# defaults the CLI prints on startup — they are public knowledge, identical on
# every machine, and reachable only from localhost.
#
# Note: this repo tracks supabase/migrations and nothing else, so there is no
# config.toml yet and `supabase start` will refuse until you run `supabase
# init` once. It also needs Docker running. If you would rather not have
# either, take Option B — the suite does not care which one it gets.
PGHOST=127.0.0.1
PGPORT=54322
PGUSER=postgres
PGPASSWORD=postgres

# --- Option B: any plain Postgres 16 ---------------------------------------
#
# No Docker needed. tests/rls/supabase-shim.sql recreates the Supabase surface
# the migrations touch (the roles, auth.users, auth.uid(), the realtime
# publication, the default grants), which is deliberate: the security tests
# must not be the ones gated behind the heaviest dependency.
#
# Homebrew (`brew install postgresql@16 && brew services start postgresql@16`)
# listens on a socket in /tmp and names its superuser after your OS user:
#   PGHOST=/tmp
#   PGPORT=5432
#   PGUSER=your-os-username
#   PGPASSWORD=

# --- Rarely needed ---------------------------------------------------------

# The existing database used to issue the CREATE/DROP for the throwaway one.
# Defaults to `postgres`, which both the Supabase stack and Homebrew provide.
# PGDATABASE=postgres

# Name of the throwaway database. Dropped and recreated on every run, so it
# must not collide with anything real.
# RLS_TEST_DB=apptest_rls

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Checking migrations against the real project
#
# Not with this file. Use the CLI, which diffs and applies rather than
# recreating:
#
#   supabase link --project-ref <ref>
#   supabase db push --dry-run     # then drop --dry-run when it looks right
#
# `supabase link` stores its own credentials under supabase/.temp, which is
# already gitignored. The database password it asks for is not an EXPO_PUBLIC_
# value and must never end up in an app .env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
